From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Morten=20Kr=C3=A5kvik?= <morten@krakvik.io>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 20:42:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] virtio/vsock: never deliver an older fwd_cnt than data
 already carried
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A credit update or request parked in rxq carries the fwd_cnt from when it
was made. Data for the same connection does not go through rxq: recv_pkt()
writes it straight into the guest's RX ring, stamped with the current
tx_cnt. So while a credit packet waits, the guest can be sent a newer
fwd_cnt in a data packet's header, and the parked packet then delivers
the older one. The guest keeps the last fwd_cnt it sees, so its view of
our buffer moves backwards, just as with the reordering fixed earlier.

Each step back is less than peer_buf_alloc / 2, but they add up. Once the
guest is behind by its own buf_alloc it has no credit, blocks in send()
with nothing unsent, and we send no further update because we believe it
already has a recent one. Both sides wait.

Keep the newest fwd_cnt given to the guest per connection in rxq, from
data and credit packets alike, and raise a credit packet to it as it is
delivered. Forget it when the connection starts or ends, since the ports
may then name a different connection.

Reproduced with libkrunfw 5.6.2 built on 6.12.115, echoing 1 MiB messages
over a guest-initiated unix-proxy port: with only the reordering fix, 2
of 10 runs stalled, and logging showed fwd_cnt going backwards 27 to 295
times per run, 572 of 579 times from a parked credit update after a data
packet. With this change, 40 of 40 runs completed (libkrunfw and a plain
6.12.115 guest), plus 15 runs with 8 GiB of pipelined echo on top.

Assisted-by: Claude Code: claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Morten Kråkvik <morten@krakvik.io>

(cherry picked from commit ae40748b, libkrun/libkrun#964, onto v1.19.8: the proxies' changes made to vsock/unix.rs and vsock/tsi_stream.rs, from before main split them into unix_proxy/ and tsi_stream/)
